1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,520
The night of April 22nd, 1951.

2
00:00:04,052 --> 00:00:06,355
Over the shallow fords of the Imjin River,

3
00:00:06,375 --> 00:00:08,915
a strange sound begins to spread.

4
00:00:09,197 --> 00:00:12,716
Gongs. Whistles. And bugles.

5
00:00:12,916 --> 00:00:15,036
Behind that sound, the footsteps

6
00:00:15,056 --> 00:00:16,504
of tens of thousands of men

7
00:00:16,524 --> 00:00:18,359
start to cut through the water.

8
00:00:18,709 --> 00:00:21,034
On the hills south of the river stood a

9
00:00:21,054 --> 00:00:24,722
British battalion of barely seven hundred men.

10
00:00:24,930 --> 00:00:27,561
Coming at them: forty thousand.

11
00:00:28,004 --> 00:00:29,623
On paper, this was never

12
00:00:29,643 --> 00:00:31,727
supposed to be a fight at all.

13
00:00:32,090 --> 00:00:33,310
And yet these seven hundred

14
00:00:33,330 --> 00:00:35,712
men held out for three days.

15
00:00:35,937 --> 00:00:37,625
And those three days would

16
00:00:37,645 --> 00:00:39,570
change the fate of Seoul.

17
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:40,989
So what kept these seven

18
00:00:41,009 --> 00:00:42,332
hundred men standing?

19
00:00:42,864 --> 00:00:45,429
No water, almost no ammunition,

20
00:00:45,449 --> 00:00:49,036
and every road back cut off behind them.

21
00:00:49,246 --> 00:00:51,730
The answer lies in a tradition one regiment

22
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:54,598
had guarded for a hundred and fifty years,

23
00:00:54,767 --> 00:00:56,090
and in the quiet decision

24
00:00:56,110 --> 00:00:59,094
of one battalion commander.

25
00:00:59,306 --> 00:01:00,812
The Battle of the Imjin River,

26
00:01:00,832 --> 00:01:03,360
and the Gloster Battalion at Solma-ri.

27
00:01:03,529 --> 00:01:06,468
Today, we follow those three days and nights,

28
00:01:06,488 --> 00:01:09,700
one move at a time, across the map.

29
00:01:10,062 --> 00:01:11,202
Before we begin,

30
00:01:11,428 --> 00:01:12,899
if you want stories like this one

31
00:01:12,919 --> 00:01:14,674
to keep being told, please

32
00:01:14,694 --> 00:01:17,679
subscribe and hit the like button.

33
00:01:17,890 --> 00:01:19,657
Let's turn the clock back a little.

34
00:01:20,007 --> 00:01:22,001
That spring, the Chinese army

35
00:01:22,021 --> 00:01:23,778
was preparing a massive offensive

36
00:01:23,798 --> 00:01:26,600
meant to flip the entire war.

37
00:01:26,814 --> 00:01:28,524
It would become known as the Chinese

38
00:01:28,544 --> 00:01:31,734
Spring Offensive of April 1951.

39
00:01:32,016 --> 00:01:32,929
More than three hundred

40
00:01:32,949 --> 00:01:34,670
thousand troops were committed.

41
00:01:34,895 --> 00:01:39,120
And the objective was one city. Seoul.

42
00:01:39,330 --> 00:01:40,960
There was even word that they

43
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:43,180
meant to retake Seoul in time

44
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:45,478
for May Day, the first of May.

45
00:01:45,648 --> 00:01:46,993
Only three months earlier,

46
00:01:47,013 --> 00:01:49,099
Seoul had already fallen to the enemy

47
00:01:49,119 --> 00:01:52,046
once more, before being won back.

48
00:01:52,259 --> 00:01:54,515
The man directing this offensive was the

49
00:01:54,535 --> 00:01:58,226
Chinese commander in Korea, Peng Dehuai.

50
00:01:58,758 --> 00:02:00,286
He massed his main strength on the

51
00:02:00,306 --> 00:02:02,495
western front, choosing to cross the

52
00:02:02,515 --> 00:02:05,681
Imjin and drive straight down on Seoul.

53
00:02:06,328 --> 00:02:07,980
That road ran through Kaesong,

54
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,666
Munsan, and Choksong.

55
00:02:10,877 --> 00:02:13,941
South of the Imjin, the UN forces held

56
00:02:13,961 --> 00:02:16,673
a defensive line called Line Kansas.

57
00:02:16,842 --> 00:02:17,743
But the troops on that

58
00:02:17,763 --> 00:02:19,689
line were spread thin,

59
00:02:19,857 --> 00:02:21,647
and no one knew exactly when,

60
00:02:21,667 --> 00:02:24,822
or where, the blow would land.

61
00:02:25,021 --> 00:02:26,913
If Seoul fell for a third time,

62
00:02:26,933 --> 00:02:29,372
the course of the war, and any hope of a

63
00:02:29,392 --> 00:02:31,875
ceasefire, could change completely.

64
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:33,720
And squarely across that invasion

65
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:36,371
route lay the Imjin River.

66
00:02:36,584 --> 00:02:38,203
The unit holding more than nineteen

67
00:02:38,223 --> 00:02:40,218
kilometers of that riverbank was

68
00:02:40,238 --> 00:02:42,744
the British 29th Infantry Brigade.

69
00:02:43,095 --> 00:02:44,270
It was an absurdly wide

70
00:02:44,290 --> 00:02:46,375
front for a single brigade.

71
00:02:46,816 --> 00:02:49,312
Normally, a distance like that would

72
00:02:49,332 --> 00:02:52,259
call for two or three times as many men.

73
00:02:52,466 --> 00:02:54,677
The brigade had three British battalions,

74
00:02:54,697 --> 00:02:56,601
the Glosters, the Northumberland

75
00:02:56,621 --> 00:02:58,718
Fusiliers and the Royal Ulster

76
00:02:58,738 --> 00:03:01,210
Rifles, plus a Belgian battalion,

77
00:03:01,379 --> 00:03:04,818
backed by artillery and Centurion tanks.

78
00:03:05,021 --> 00:03:07,778
The problem was the space in between.

79
00:03:08,129 --> 00:03:10,066
Between one battalion and the next,

80
00:03:10,086 --> 00:03:13,366
there were gaps several kilometers wide.

81
00:03:13,573 --> 00:03:16,091
At the far left end of that line, guarding

82
00:03:16,111 --> 00:03:18,959
the road down to Seoul, stood one unit.

83
00:03:19,185 --> 00:03:20,428
The 1st Battalion,

84
00:03:20,448 --> 00:03:24,058
the Gloucestershire Regiment. The Glosters.

85
00:03:24,267 --> 00:03:26,592
So what happens if this battalion breaks?

86
00:03:26,942 --> 00:03:28,288
The enemy pours straight down

87
00:03:28,308 --> 00:03:30,837
toward Uijeongbu and Seoul.

88
00:03:31,044 --> 00:03:32,503
And the flank of every other British

89
00:03:32,523 --> 00:03:35,656
battalion to their right is laid wide open.

90
00:03:36,007 --> 00:03:38,468
One battalion, in effect, was holding

91
00:03:38,488 --> 00:03:41,393
the door handle of the entire front.

92
00:03:41,597 --> 00:03:42,875
The Glosters carry one

93
00:03:42,895 --> 00:03:44,581
very unusual tradition.

94
00:03:45,023 --> 00:03:46,540
Their soldiers wear the regimental

95
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:48,384
badge not only on the front of

96
00:03:48,404 --> 00:03:51,354
their caps, but on the back as well.

97
00:03:51,564 --> 00:03:53,820
It honors a battle in Egypt a hundred

98
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:56,109
and fifty years earlier, when the regiment

99
00:03:56,129 --> 00:03:58,510
was attacked from front and rear at once,

100
00:03:58,530 --> 00:04:01,241
and fought back to back, and won.

101
00:04:01,467 --> 00:04:04,132
Enemy in front. Enemy behind.

102
00:04:04,414 --> 00:04:06,727
No one could have imagined that badge

103
00:04:06,747 --> 00:04:09,743
would become reality again on the Imjin.

104
00:04:09,952 --> 00:04:11,809
Keep that badge in mind for a moment.

105
00:04:12,251 --> 00:04:13,426
Because in this battle,

106
00:04:13,446 --> 00:04:16,658
it stops being just a decoration.

107
00:04:16,872 --> 00:04:18,355
The battalion commander was

108
00:04:18,375 --> 00:04:20,516
Lieutenant Colonel James Carne.

109
00:04:20,867 --> 00:04:23,044
A man of few words, he was known

110
00:04:23,064 --> 00:04:24,489
for the pipe he always seemed

111
00:04:24,509 --> 00:04:26,583
to have clenched in his teeth.

112
00:04:26,796 --> 00:04:29,154
The battalion had arrived in Korea the

113
00:04:29,174 --> 00:04:31,443
previous autumn, and had already been

114
00:04:31,463 --> 00:04:33,559
through a brutal winter of fighting.

115
00:04:34,092 --> 00:04:35,961
Under his command were roughly

116
00:04:35,981 --> 00:04:38,465
seven hundred and fifty men.

117
00:04:38,676 --> 00:04:40,682
They had set foot in Korea as the

118
00:04:40,702 --> 00:04:43,481
winter of 1950 was closing in.

119
00:04:43,650 --> 00:04:45,201
In temperatures that swung around

120
00:04:45,221 --> 00:04:47,512
twenty below zero, they had already

121
00:04:47,532 --> 00:04:49,606
fallen back once to south of Seoul,

122
00:04:49,626 --> 00:04:52,758
and then fought their way north again.

123
00:04:52,964 --> 00:04:55,129
The battalion dug in company by company

124
00:04:55,149 --> 00:04:57,417
across the hills around Choksong in

125
00:04:57,437 --> 00:05:00,501
Paju, on the slopes of Mount Kamak.

126
00:05:00,852 --> 00:05:03,017
A Company took the hill jutting out

127
00:05:03,037 --> 00:05:06,181
toward the river, known as Castle Hill.

128
00:05:06,391 --> 00:05:08,158
And behind battalion headquarters

129
00:05:08,178 --> 00:05:10,388
rose one small peak.

130
00:05:10,830 --> 00:05:13,120
Two hundred and thirty-five meters high.

131
00:05:13,653 --> 00:05:17,013
It would later be known as Gloster Hill.

132
00:05:17,214 --> 00:05:18,776
Cut a cross-section through the Imjin

133
00:05:18,796 --> 00:05:20,836
valley that April, and you can see

134
00:05:20,856 --> 00:05:22,862
why this ground was so dangerous.

135
00:05:23,087 --> 00:05:24,559
The river is low, and the

136
00:05:24,579 --> 00:05:26,379
hills are not very high.

137
00:05:26,594 --> 00:05:29,191
But this layout had a fatal weakness.

138
00:05:29,473 --> 00:05:31,843
The companies were simply too far apart.

139
00:05:32,125 --> 00:05:34,394
In places, they were too far apart

140
00:05:34,414 --> 00:05:36,943
to cover one another with fire.

141
00:05:37,151 --> 00:05:40,192
With so few men, there was no other choice.

142
00:05:40,634 --> 00:05:43,027
Colonel Carne knew about the gaps,

143
00:05:43,047 --> 00:05:45,678
but he had no one left to fill them.

144
00:05:45,884 --> 00:05:47,571
Beyond the battalion's left flank

145
00:05:47,591 --> 00:05:50,222
stood the South Korean 1st Division.

146
00:05:50,449 --> 00:05:52,432
But between those two units, too,

147
00:05:52,452 --> 00:05:55,197
lay a wide gap of hills and valleys.

148
00:05:55,548 --> 00:05:57,098
Each held its own ground,

149
00:05:57,118 --> 00:06:00,011
without ever quite joining hands.

150
00:06:00,213 --> 00:06:02,139
And then there was the river

151
00:06:02,159 --> 00:06:04,472
running in front of the battalion.

152
00:06:04,823 --> 00:06:07,693
In April, the Imjin runs shallow.

153
00:06:07,919 --> 00:06:10,072
At the fords, the water came up

154
00:06:10,092 --> 00:06:12,565
no higher than a man's waist.

155
00:06:12,776 --> 00:06:14,566
The river was no obstacle at all.

156
00:06:14,586 --> 00:06:17,331
It was simply a road you could walk across.

157
00:06:17,556 --> 00:06:18,561
The soldiers called that

158
00:06:18,581 --> 00:06:21,303
spot Gloster Crossing.

159
00:06:21,509 --> 00:06:23,492
Among the men were hardened

160
00:06:23,512 --> 00:06:25,882
veterans of the Second World War,

161
00:06:26,107 --> 00:06:28,250
but many were young conscripts of about

162
00:06:28,270 --> 00:06:31,323
twenty, freshly shipped across the sea.

163
00:06:31,970 --> 00:06:34,636
These rocky, unfamiliar mountains

164
00:06:34,656 --> 00:06:36,446
were nothing like the green

165
00:06:36,466 --> 00:06:39,006
fields of home in Gloucestershire.

166
00:06:39,218 --> 00:06:41,531
That April, azaleas were blooming

167
00:06:41,551 --> 00:06:43,420
on the slopes of Mount Kamak.

168
00:06:43,862 --> 00:06:47,393
By day the men dug trenches, and by night

169
00:06:47,413 --> 00:06:50,671
they sent patrols across toward the far bank.

170
00:06:51,203 --> 00:06:53,619
For days, the patrols found not

171
00:06:53,639 --> 00:06:56,395
so much as a shadow of the enemy.

172
00:06:56,607 --> 00:06:59,819
For days, the front was strangely quiet.

173
00:07:00,170 --> 00:07:01,447
The darkness across the river

174
00:07:01,467 --> 00:07:03,906
already knew that this calm was

175
00:07:03,926 --> 00:07:07,092
the silence before the storm.

176
00:07:07,453 --> 00:07:09,732
The night of April 22nd.

177
00:07:09,752 --> 00:07:12,793
Moonlight was shining on the river.

178
00:07:13,003 --> 00:07:15,271
Near the ford lay a platoon-sized ambush

179
00:07:15,291 --> 00:07:18,002
patrol led by Lieutenant Guy Temple.

180
00:07:18,353 --> 00:07:19,709
As midnight drew near,

181
00:07:19,729 --> 00:07:22,486
dark shapes appeared on the water.

182
00:07:22,698 --> 00:07:24,214
Temple's patrol is said to have

183
00:07:24,234 --> 00:07:25,603
stopped the enemy crossing in

184
00:07:25,623 --> 00:07:28,266
midstream several times that night.

185
00:07:28,549 --> 00:07:29,872
But this was not an enemy

186
00:07:29,892 --> 00:07:32,819
one platoon could hold back.

187
00:07:33,025 --> 00:07:34,723
It wasn't one or two men.

188
00:07:35,074 --> 00:07:36,420
They came in lines,

189
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:38,911
wading across without end.

190
00:07:39,123 --> 00:07:41,481
The patrol opened fire all at once.

191
00:07:41,832 --> 00:07:44,281
The leading ranks fell into the river.

192
00:07:44,487 --> 00:07:46,140
And then something strange.

193
00:07:46,581 --> 00:07:48,462
The next rank walked straight over

194
00:07:48,482 --> 00:07:50,499
the spot where the first had fallen.

195
00:07:50,781 --> 00:07:53,607
They fell, and still more came.

196
00:07:53,810 --> 00:07:55,839
Temple's platoon only pulled back after

197
00:07:55,859 --> 00:07:58,673
firing until its ammunition ran out.

198
00:07:59,114 --> 00:08:00,540
The enemy who crossed the river that

199
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:03,760
night was the Chinese 63rd Army.

200
00:08:03,985 --> 00:08:07,664
Three divisions. Tens of thousands of men.

201
00:08:07,865 --> 00:08:10,121
The 63rd Army's orders were clear.

202
00:08:10,403 --> 00:08:12,432
Cross the Imjin, smash through the

203
00:08:12,452 --> 00:08:14,094
British positions in one blow,

204
00:08:14,114 --> 00:08:16,711
and throw open the road to Uijeongbu.

205
00:08:16,993 --> 00:08:18,829
By their calculations, the whole

206
00:08:18,849 --> 00:08:20,991
job would take a single night.

207
00:08:21,195 --> 00:08:23,325
And that is when the sound begins.

208
00:08:23,676 --> 00:08:26,217
Gongs and cymbals, shrill whistles,

209
00:08:26,237 --> 00:08:29,039
and long, wailing bugles.

210
00:08:29,254 --> 00:08:32,022
The Chinese used these sounds to signal the

211
00:08:32,042 --> 00:08:34,572
direction of attack, and at the same time

212
00:08:34,592 --> 00:08:36,974
to grind down the nerves of the enemy.

213
00:08:37,415 --> 00:08:39,342
Noise echoing from every side,

214
00:08:39,362 --> 00:08:41,491
below a pitch-black ridge.

215
00:08:41,775 --> 00:08:43,257
The terror of not knowing where

216
00:08:43,277 --> 00:08:46,068
they were coming from, or how many.

217
00:08:46,274 --> 00:08:48,086
And the attack that night was

218
00:08:48,106 --> 00:08:50,066
not aimed at the Glosters alone.

219
00:08:50,508 --> 00:08:52,799
The Belgian battalion, posted north of

220
00:08:52,819 --> 00:08:55,838
the river, was cut off, and the Fusiliers

221
00:08:55,858 --> 00:08:58,490
on the right were hit all night long.

222
00:08:59,022 --> 00:09:00,948
The whole brigade was engulfed

223
00:09:00,968 --> 00:09:02,997
in flames at the same time.

224
00:09:03,209 --> 00:09:04,896
The British artillery poured shells

225
00:09:04,916 --> 00:09:06,876
out all night without a pause.

226
00:09:07,409 --> 00:09:11,099
It's said the gun barrels glowed red-hot.

227
00:09:11,312 --> 00:09:13,750
Still the waves did not shrink.

228
00:09:13,919 --> 00:09:16,027
Cut down one battalion, and another

229
00:09:16,047 --> 00:09:17,961
battalion stood right behind it.

230
00:09:18,130 --> 00:09:21,251
This was the Chinese human-wave attack.

231
00:09:21,467 --> 00:09:24,338
The first Chinese blade struck the most

232
00:09:24,358 --> 00:09:27,889
exposed position, A Company on Castle Hill.

233
00:09:28,239 --> 00:09:29,950
Grenades fell like rain,

234
00:09:29,970 --> 00:09:33,318
and bayonets clashed between the trenches.

235
00:09:33,529 --> 00:09:35,684
Through that long night, the positions on

236
00:09:35,704 --> 00:09:38,266
the hill changed hands again and again.

237
00:09:38,492 --> 00:09:40,407
By daybreak, A Company had

238
00:09:40,427 --> 00:09:42,830
lost nearly half its men.

239
00:09:43,046 --> 00:09:44,903
And this was only the beginning.

240
00:09:45,129 --> 00:09:48,842
The real ordeal would come after sunrise.

241
00:09:49,056 --> 00:09:51,221
The morning of April 23rd.

242
00:09:51,446 --> 00:09:53,816
A Company's commander, Major Pat

243
00:09:53,836 --> 00:09:56,331
Angier, is killed on the hill.

244
00:09:56,614 --> 00:09:57,903
With its commander gone,

245
00:09:57,923 --> 00:10:01,043
the company was on the verge of collapse.

246
00:10:01,252 --> 00:10:04,578
By a cruel coincidence, it was Saint George's Day,

247
00:10:04,598 --> 00:10:07,093
the feast of England's patron saint.

248
00:10:07,262 --> 00:10:08,950
At home, it was a day for

249
00:10:08,970 --> 00:10:10,792
wearing a rose on your chest,

250
00:10:10,916 --> 00:10:12,113
but up on that hill,

251
00:10:12,133 --> 00:10:14,742
no one had a moment to mark it.

252
00:10:14,954 --> 00:10:16,766
Then a young officer pulls

253
00:10:16,786 --> 00:10:18,029
himself to his feet.

254
00:10:18,255 --> 00:10:20,385
Lieutenant Philip Curtis.

255
00:10:20,586 --> 00:10:22,114
At the top of Castle Hill,

256
00:10:22,134 --> 00:10:24,936
the Chinese had seized a machine-gun post.

257
00:10:25,161 --> 00:10:27,088
That one position was pinning

258
00:10:27,108 --> 00:10:29,387
down the entire company.

259
00:10:29,587 --> 00:10:31,467
Curtis had already been shot.

260
00:10:31,818 --> 00:10:33,346
His men tried to hold him back,

261
00:10:33,366 --> 00:10:35,998
but he shook them off and charged up

262
00:10:36,018 --> 00:10:39,298
the slope alone, grenades in hand.

263
00:10:39,658 --> 00:10:41,664
Hit again, he went down,

264
00:10:41,684 --> 00:10:43,587
then got up and crawled on.

265
00:10:43,813 --> 00:10:46,058
And at the moment he threw his last grenade

266
00:10:46,078 --> 00:10:49,096
into the post, he died on the spot.

267
00:10:49,306 --> 00:10:51,027
He would be awarded Britain's highest

268
00:10:51,047 --> 00:10:54,248
decoration for valor, the Victoria Cross.

269
00:10:54,599 --> 00:10:56,537
It was the final charge of a

270
00:10:56,557 --> 00:10:58,483
twenty-four-year-old officer.

271
00:10:58,833 --> 00:11:01,500
And his sacrifice bought A Company the

272
00:11:01,520 --> 00:11:04,435
time it needed to get off the hill.

273
00:11:04,639 --> 00:11:06,508
When daylight came, the Chinese

274
00:11:06,528 --> 00:11:08,295
attacks paused for breath.

275
00:11:08,646 --> 00:11:10,811
British guns and UN fighter-bombers

276
00:11:10,831 --> 00:11:12,927
owned the daytime sky.

277
00:11:13,153 --> 00:11:15,432
But even then, the enemy never stopped

278
00:11:15,452 --> 00:11:18,083
moving through the hills and valleys.

279
00:11:18,285 --> 00:11:20,519
But the real danger was not in front

280
00:11:20,539 --> 00:11:22,908
of the hill. It was behind it.

281
00:11:23,259 --> 00:11:24,685
The Chinese weren't only

282
00:11:24,705 --> 00:11:26,539
hammering the front.

283
00:11:26,745 --> 00:11:28,285
Thousands of them had slipped through

284
00:11:28,305 --> 00:11:31,141
those wide gaps between the battalions,

285
00:11:31,310 --> 00:11:33,031
and come all the way down into the

286
00:11:33,051 --> 00:11:36,502
Solma-ri valley, behind the Glosters.

287
00:11:36,710 --> 00:11:38,135
The supply route was cut.

288
00:11:38,155 --> 00:11:40,195
The way out was blocked.

289
00:11:40,421 --> 00:11:43,109
Enemy in front. Enemy behind.

290
00:11:43,335 --> 00:11:44,862
The legend of the badge on the back

291
00:11:44,882 --> 00:11:47,878
of their caps had come to life.

292
00:11:48,086 --> 00:11:49,933
The only link left between brigade

293
00:11:49,953 --> 00:11:51,401
headquarters and the battalion

294
00:11:51,421 --> 00:11:53,813
was a thin thread of radio.

295
00:11:54,096 --> 00:11:56,352
And over that radio, the battalion's

296
00:11:56,372 --> 00:11:59,391
situation grew worse by the hour.

297
00:11:59,598 --> 00:12:01,490
That night, the remaining companies

298
00:12:01,510 --> 00:12:03,436
were attacked one after another.

299
00:12:03,719 --> 00:12:06,111
On the ridges held by D and B Companies,

300
00:12:06,131 --> 00:12:07,363
grenades and machine

301
00:12:07,383 --> 00:12:09,787
guns never fell silent all night.

302
00:12:10,013 --> 00:12:12,110
And with every dawn, the empty places

303
00:12:12,130 --> 00:12:14,454
in the trenches grew more obvious.

304
00:12:14,661 --> 00:12:16,587
Colonel Carne made his decision.

305
00:12:16,755 --> 00:12:18,249
He would pull in all his scattered

306
00:12:18,269 --> 00:12:20,924
companies and gather them in one place.

307
00:12:21,149 --> 00:12:24,690
That place was Hill 235.

308
00:12:24,903 --> 00:12:26,362
Just getting the companies up to

309
00:12:26,382 --> 00:12:28,684
the hill was a battle in itself.

310
00:12:29,034 --> 00:12:30,984
They carried their wounded on stretchers

311
00:12:31,004 --> 00:12:33,682
and crawled up the ridges, loaded

312
00:12:33,702 --> 00:12:36,754
with heavy mortars and machine guns.

313
00:12:36,964 --> 00:12:38,321
Men who fell behind in the

314
00:12:38,341 --> 00:12:40,574
darkness never came back.

315
00:12:40,857 --> 00:12:42,168
And the battalion that gathered

316
00:12:42,188 --> 00:12:44,001
there now had nowhere left

317
00:12:44,021 --> 00:12:46,903
to retreat, not a single step.

318
00:12:47,111 --> 00:12:48,844
The Glosters had become an island,

319
00:12:48,864 --> 00:12:51,155
surrounded by the enemy on all sides,

320
00:12:51,175 --> 00:12:54,455
a full three hundred and sixty degrees.

321
00:12:54,818 --> 00:12:56,345
April 24th.

322
00:12:56,993 --> 00:12:58,406
The men gathered on the hill

323
00:12:58,426 --> 00:13:01,706
numbered barely four hundred.

324
00:13:01,905 --> 00:13:04,025
After two straight nights of fighting,

325
00:13:04,045 --> 00:13:05,936
their canteens were empty.

326
00:13:06,378 --> 00:13:07,645
The ammunition boxes were

327
00:13:07,665 --> 00:13:09,886
starting to show their bottoms.

328
00:13:10,084 --> 00:13:12,374
Even the radio batteries were running down.

329
00:13:12,656 --> 00:13:14,185
Once that last link to brigade

330
00:13:14,205 --> 00:13:16,187
went dead, the battalion would be

331
00:13:16,207 --> 00:13:18,475
truly cut off from the world.

332
00:13:18,684 --> 00:13:21,032
The wounded lay on the floors of the trenches,

333
00:13:21,052 --> 00:13:23,945
waiting for a single mouthful of water.

334
00:13:24,296 --> 00:13:25,778
And the path down to the valley

335
00:13:25,798 --> 00:13:28,624
to fetch that water was already

336
00:13:28,644 --> 00:13:31,628
inside the enemy's field of fire.

337
00:13:31,839 --> 00:13:33,595
The men counted out what ammunition was

338
00:13:33,615 --> 00:13:36,633
left and shared it, round by round.

339
00:13:36,916 --> 00:13:38,569
So many rounds per rifle,

340
00:13:38,589 --> 00:13:40,776
so many belts per machine gun.

341
00:13:41,059 --> 00:13:43,235
Not one shot could be wasted.

342
00:13:43,442 --> 00:13:45,072
They had to wait until the enemy was

343
00:13:45,092 --> 00:13:47,030
right on top of them, and pull the

344
00:13:47,050 --> 00:13:49,363
trigger only when they could not miss.

345
00:13:49,645 --> 00:13:51,002
Hunger and thirst,

346
00:13:51,022 --> 00:13:53,700
and a third night without sleep.

347
00:13:53,907 --> 00:13:56,402
Their eyes were already hollow and sunken.

348
00:13:56,752 --> 00:13:59,566
And yet not one of them left his trench.

349
00:13:59,779 --> 00:14:02,126
Some companies never made it up to the hill.

350
00:14:02,351 --> 00:14:04,790
B Company, holding a separate ridge,

351
00:14:04,810 --> 00:14:07,169
beat off several attacks through the night,

352
00:14:07,189 --> 00:14:09,445
but in the end its ammunition

353
00:14:09,465 --> 00:14:12,268
ran out and its position was overrun.

354
00:14:12,619 --> 00:14:14,306
It is said that only a handful of its

355
00:14:14,326 --> 00:14:17,959
men made it up to Hill 235 alive.

356
00:14:18,167 --> 00:14:19,616
With the battalion was a troop

357
00:14:19,636 --> 00:14:21,447
of British mortars as well.

358
00:14:21,730 --> 00:14:24,055
They too kept firing to the very end,

359
00:14:24,075 --> 00:14:27,013
and shared the Glosters' fate.

360
00:14:27,224 --> 00:14:28,513
From the rear, there was

361
00:14:28,533 --> 00:14:29,969
an attempt to save them.

362
00:14:30,320 --> 00:14:32,132
A Filipino battalion pushed into the

363
00:14:32,152 --> 00:14:35,569
Solma-ri valley together with British tanks.

364
00:14:35,786 --> 00:14:37,997
The Filipinos had come from a distant

365
00:14:38,017 --> 00:14:40,353
island nation far to the south.

366
00:14:40,794 --> 00:14:43,393
They too threw themselves into that gorge to

367
00:14:43,413 --> 00:14:46,635
rescue British soldiers in a foreign land.

368
00:14:46,847 --> 00:14:48,649
But the narrow gorge road was

369
00:14:48,669 --> 00:14:50,446
a Chinese killing ground.

370
00:14:50,672 --> 00:14:52,382
When the lead tank was knocked

371
00:14:52,402 --> 00:14:54,031
out and blocked the road,

372
00:14:54,200 --> 00:14:55,626
the relief force never reached

373
00:14:55,646 --> 00:14:58,334
the hill, and had to turn back.

374
00:14:58,548 --> 00:14:59,849
There is a painful story

375
00:14:59,869 --> 00:15:01,396
told about this moment.

376
00:15:01,678 --> 00:15:04,014
Brigadier Tom Brodie, in classic

377
00:15:04,034 --> 00:15:06,268
British understatement, is said to

378
00:15:06,288 --> 00:15:08,465
have told his American superior that

379
00:15:08,485 --> 00:15:11,992
things were, quote, a bit sticky.

380
00:15:12,203 --> 00:15:13,492
That restrained phrase,

381
00:15:13,512 --> 00:15:16,121
the story goes, never conveyed how

382
00:15:16,141 --> 00:15:18,226
serious the crisis really was.

383
00:15:18,578 --> 00:15:21,437
Meanwhile, up on the hill, an entire

384
00:15:21,457 --> 00:15:24,145
battalion was on the edge of disappearing.

385
00:15:24,356 --> 00:15:27,568
Still, Colonel Carne did not waver.

386
00:15:27,793 --> 00:15:29,560
He is said to have strolled calmly

387
00:15:29,580 --> 00:15:32,121
between the trenches as shells fell,

388
00:15:32,141 --> 00:15:34,181
patting his men on the shoulder.

389
00:15:34,464 --> 00:15:36,071
As long as their commander was on his

390
00:15:36,091 --> 00:15:39,667
feet, his men could not fall either.

391
00:15:39,870 --> 00:15:42,183
That night, the Chinese came again.

392
00:15:42,465 --> 00:15:45,028
Mortar rounds plowed up the ridgeline,

393
00:15:45,242 --> 00:15:47,077
and the men fought with bayonets,

394
00:15:47,097 --> 00:15:49,945
rifle butts, and even rocks.

395
00:15:50,152 --> 00:15:52,658
Then, at dawn on April 25th,

396
00:15:52,827 --> 00:15:55,140
the battalion adjutant, Captain Anthony

397
00:15:55,160 --> 00:15:57,530
Farrar-Hockley, called for Drum Major

398
00:15:57,550 --> 00:16:00,068
Philip Buss and gave him an order.

399
00:16:00,510 --> 00:16:02,448
Sound the bugle. Every call

400
00:16:02,468 --> 00:16:05,190
you know, except the retreat.

401
00:16:05,405 --> 00:16:06,854
Over the smoke-covered hill,

402
00:16:06,874 --> 00:16:08,833
a British bugle rang out.

403
00:16:09,002 --> 00:16:11,838
A bugle, answering the gongs.

404
00:16:12,050 --> 00:16:14,032
A cheer burst out among the men.

405
00:16:14,258 --> 00:16:17,572
It was their answer: we are still here.

406
00:16:17,786 --> 00:16:20,053
The morning of April 25th.

407
00:16:20,586 --> 00:16:22,262
Over the radio, the final

408
00:16:22,282 --> 00:16:24,037
message came through.

409
00:16:24,252 --> 00:16:25,916
There would be no more relief.

410
00:16:26,198 --> 00:16:28,204
Artillery support would soon end.

411
00:16:28,430 --> 00:16:30,367
Find your own way out.

412
00:16:30,579 --> 00:16:32,710
In effect, it was the last order

413
00:16:32,730 --> 00:16:35,054
the battalion would ever receive.

414
00:16:35,266 --> 00:16:38,012
That morning, UN fighter-bombers swept in

415
00:16:38,032 --> 00:16:40,982
around the hill with machine guns and napalm.

416
00:16:41,264 --> 00:16:43,920
It was cover, meant to open even the

417
00:16:43,940 --> 00:16:47,436
smallest gap for one last escape.

418
00:16:47,649 --> 00:16:48,733
Colonel Carne called his

419
00:16:48,753 --> 00:16:50,440
company commanders together.

420
00:16:50,494 --> 00:16:52,545
And he told them, calmly,

421
00:16:52,669 --> 00:16:55,574
from here on, each of you use your own judgment,

422
00:16:55,594 --> 00:16:58,908
break through, and make for our lines.

423
00:16:59,122 --> 00:17:01,196
If the battalion moved as one body,

424
00:17:01,216 --> 00:17:03,689
it could be wiped out all at once.

425
00:17:04,130 --> 00:17:05,863
Only by splitting up could

426
00:17:05,883 --> 00:17:07,581
anyone hope to make it home.

427
00:17:08,228 --> 00:17:09,687
Men who had held out together

428
00:17:09,707 --> 00:17:11,816
for three days now had to stake

429
00:17:11,836 --> 00:17:14,615
their lives on different paths.

430
00:17:14,825 --> 00:17:16,466
But not everyone could leave.

431
00:17:16,748 --> 00:17:19,802
There were men too badly wounded to move.

432
00:17:20,007 --> 00:17:22,503
The medical officer, Captain Bob Hickey,

433
00:17:22,523 --> 00:17:24,642
and the chaplain, the Reverend Sam

434
00:17:24,662 --> 00:17:27,236
Davies, chose to stay on the hill.

435
00:17:27,405 --> 00:17:29,627
They would remain beside the wounded.

436
00:17:29,839 --> 00:17:32,026
Even though it meant certain capture.

437
00:17:32,673 --> 00:17:34,679
A doctor and a priest gave up

438
00:17:34,699 --> 00:17:37,183
their own chance to escape.

439
00:17:37,390 --> 00:17:38,531
And to guard the backs of the

440
00:17:38,551 --> 00:17:40,021
comrades who were leaving,

441
00:17:40,145 --> 00:17:42,288
some men stayed on the ridge, gripping

442
00:17:42,308 --> 00:17:45,508
their machine guns, to the very end.

443
00:17:45,723 --> 00:17:48,207
Shortly after ten in the morning, the men

444
00:17:48,227 --> 00:17:51,018
began going down the hill in small groups.

445
00:17:51,244 --> 00:17:53,648
As they half ran, half slid down

446
00:17:53,668 --> 00:17:55,878
the steep slopes, Chinese machine

447
00:17:55,898 --> 00:17:58,349
guns opened up behind them.

448
00:17:58,559 --> 00:18:00,245
The breakout was desperate.

449
00:18:00,596 --> 00:18:02,159
Most of the men headed south,

450
00:18:02,179 --> 00:18:04,252
and were captured by the Chinese.

451
00:18:04,694 --> 00:18:08,134
Colonel Carne, too, was taken prisoner.

452
00:18:08,337 --> 00:18:11,299
So was the adjutant, Captain Farrar-Hockley.

453
00:18:11,740 --> 00:18:13,564
In the prison camps, he tried

454
00:18:13,584 --> 00:18:15,556
to escape again and again,

455
00:18:15,678 --> 00:18:18,470
and he later rose to become a British Army general,

456
00:18:18,490 --> 00:18:21,190
and wrote the record of this battle.

457
00:18:21,404 --> 00:18:24,537
Only one group, led by D Company's commander,

458
00:18:24,557 --> 00:18:27,917
Major Mike Harvey, chose a different path.

459
00:18:28,199 --> 00:18:30,376
Instead of going south, where the enemy

460
00:18:30,396 --> 00:18:32,846
expected, they first turned north,

461
00:18:32,866 --> 00:18:36,169
swinging in a wide arc over the mountains.

462
00:18:36,377 --> 00:18:38,895
Even that final stretch was not easy.

463
00:18:39,177 --> 00:18:41,035
When the exhausted men came down into

464
00:18:41,055 --> 00:18:43,221
a valley, friendly tanks mistook

465
00:18:43,241 --> 00:18:46,316
them for the enemy and opened fire.

466
00:18:46,528 --> 00:18:49,159
Some fell on the very threshold of safety.

467
00:18:49,442 --> 00:18:50,970
Only after a friendly spotter

468
00:18:50,990 --> 00:18:53,246
plane circling overhead recognized

469
00:18:53,266 --> 00:18:55,386
them and signaled the tanks,

470
00:18:55,441 --> 00:18:58,015
were they able to come home alive.

471
00:18:58,218 --> 00:19:00,702
Of that group, only about forty men

472
00:19:00,722 --> 00:19:03,649
finally reached friendly lines.

473
00:19:04,010 --> 00:19:06,062
A battalion that began with

474
00:19:06,082 --> 00:19:08,258
seven hundred and fifty men.

475
00:19:08,790 --> 00:19:10,523
Those who broke through and made

476
00:19:10,543 --> 00:19:14,085
it back numbered only a few dozen.

477
00:19:14,297 --> 00:19:15,313
More than five hundred

478
00:19:15,333 --> 00:19:16,849
men were taken prisoner,

479
00:19:17,018 --> 00:19:18,739
and many of them would spend

480
00:19:18,759 --> 00:19:20,902
more than two years in prison camps,

481
00:19:20,922 --> 00:19:23,508
until the war was over.

482
00:19:23,717 --> 00:19:25,290
The prisoners were marched hundreds of

483
00:19:25,310 --> 00:19:28,704
kilometers north, to camps near the Yalu River.

484
00:19:28,987 --> 00:19:30,481
Through hunger and cold,

485
00:19:30,501 --> 00:19:33,246
more than a few never came home.

486
00:19:33,600 --> 00:19:35,446
So were these three days a defeat?

487
00:19:35,728 --> 00:19:37,086
Judged by the numbers alone,

488
00:19:37,106 --> 00:19:39,122
it might look that way.

489
00:19:39,330 --> 00:19:42,542
But in those three days, the Chinese 63rd

490
00:19:42,562 --> 00:19:45,558
Army's timetable fell completely apart.

491
00:19:46,091 --> 00:19:47,927
The fastest road to Seoul had

492
00:19:47,947 --> 00:19:50,680
been blocked by one small hill.

493
00:19:50,894 --> 00:19:53,321
The whole British 29th Brigade had

494
00:19:53,341 --> 00:19:55,449
faced tens of thousands of men,

495
00:19:55,572 --> 00:19:57,783
and some estimates put Chinese

496
00:19:57,803 --> 00:20:00,469
losses as high as ten thousand.

497
00:20:00,683 --> 00:20:02,336
And the Glosters were not

498
00:20:02,356 --> 00:20:03,895
the only ones who bled.

499
00:20:04,428 --> 00:20:06,525
That same day, the Fusiliers and

500
00:20:06,545 --> 00:20:08,779
the Ulsters also had to fight their

501
00:20:08,799 --> 00:20:10,781
way back down narrow valleys.

502
00:20:11,223 --> 00:20:12,648
Over those three days,

503
00:20:12,668 --> 00:20:15,756
the brigade's killed, wounded and missing

504
00:20:15,776 --> 00:20:18,408
came to more than a thousand.

505
00:20:18,609 --> 00:20:20,206
But the weight of those three days

506
00:20:20,226 --> 00:20:22,697
cannot be measured in numbers alone.

507
00:20:23,139 --> 00:20:25,635
Had this line broken on the very first night,

508
00:20:25,655 --> 00:20:27,456
the Chinese might have reached the

509
00:20:27,476 --> 00:20:30,870
gates of Uijeongbu within a day or two.

510
00:20:31,076 --> 00:20:32,377
In the time they bought,

511
00:20:32,397 --> 00:20:35,154
UN forces were able to build a solid new

512
00:20:35,174 --> 00:20:37,532
defensive line north of Seoul.

513
00:20:37,883 --> 00:20:40,412
And the Chinese Spring Offensive finally

514
00:20:40,432 --> 00:20:43,747
ground to a halt at Seoul's doorstep.

515
00:20:43,951 --> 00:20:46,344
In captivity, Colonel Carne is said to

516
00:20:46,364 --> 00:20:48,585
have carved a small cross out of stone.

517
00:20:48,869 --> 00:20:50,874
That cross rests today in

518
00:20:50,894 --> 00:20:53,480
Gloucester Cathedral, in England.

519
00:20:53,685 --> 00:20:55,201
He reportedly spent much of his

520
00:20:55,221 --> 00:20:57,830
captivity in solitary confinement.

521
00:20:58,181 --> 00:21:00,540
Only after the armistice in 1953

522
00:21:00,560 --> 00:21:03,396
was he finally able to go home.

523
00:21:03,608 --> 00:21:05,750
He was awarded the Victoria Cross,

524
00:21:05,839 --> 00:21:07,800
and the whole battalion received the U.S.

525
00:21:07,820 --> 00:21:09,699
Presidential Unit Citation.

526
00:21:10,233 --> 00:21:12,420
To this day, the regiment's successors

527
00:21:12,440 --> 00:21:15,595
wear that citation on their sleeves.

528
00:21:15,804 --> 00:21:18,959
In Solma-ri, Paju, a British battle

529
00:21:18,979 --> 00:21:22,043
memorial still stands in that valley today.

530
00:21:22,394 --> 00:21:24,708
Every April, white-haired veterans

531
00:21:24,728 --> 00:21:26,359
and their families gathered

532
00:21:26,379 --> 00:21:29,294
before it and bowed their heads.

533
00:21:29,506 --> 00:21:31,592
Decades after the war, the old

534
00:21:31,612 --> 00:21:33,049
soldiers still came back to this

535
00:21:33,069 --> 00:21:35,404
valley, leaning on their canes.

536
00:21:35,629 --> 00:21:37,784
For them, this was the ground where they had

537
00:21:37,804 --> 00:21:41,221
buried their friends and left them behind.

538
00:21:41,432 --> 00:21:43,643
Paju, too, has held a memorial

539
00:21:43,663 --> 00:21:46,476
ceremony here year after year.

540
00:21:46,918 --> 00:21:49,311
Korean soldiers and citizens have

541
00:21:49,331 --> 00:21:51,155
laid flowers in the same place,

542
00:21:51,175 --> 00:21:54,740
side by side with the British veterans.

543
00:21:54,950 --> 00:21:56,990
Today, nearly all the survivors

544
00:21:57,010 --> 00:21:58,344
of those days are gone.

545
00:21:58,786 --> 00:22:00,302
But their names are still

546
00:22:00,322 --> 00:22:02,384
carved into the stone.

547
00:22:02,746 --> 00:22:04,433
Seven hundred men who fought back

548
00:22:04,453 --> 00:22:06,106
to back on the bank of a strange

549
00:22:06,126 --> 00:22:08,212
river in a faraway country,

550
